# Political Philosophy: Justice, Rights, and the State

> Why obey the state at all? Political philosophy asks what makes
> power legitimate, what justice demands of institutions, and how
> much liberty a society may trade for security or equality. This
> lesson maps the classic answers and their modern forms.

## 1. The Questions of Political Philosophy

Three questions organise the field. **Who should rule?** — the
question of *authority*. **When must obedience give way to
resistance?** — the question of *legitimacy*. **What may the state
rightly do?** — the question of its *scope*. Every position in this
lesson is an answer to some mixture of these.

| Concept | Question | Danger if ignored |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Authority | Who may command? | Might becomes right |
| Legitimacy | Why should anyone obey? | Rule rests on fear alone |
| Scope | What may the state do? | Liberty or justice erodes |

[definition]
A state is **legitimate** when its subjects have *reasons* to obey
beyond fear of punishment — reasons that would survive open debate.
Illegitimate power may still win; it simply has no moral claim to
obedience.
[/definition]

## 2. The Social Contract: Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau

**Social contract theory** grounds the state in agreement: politics
is legitimate because free and equal persons *would have consented*
to it. Each thinker starts from a "state of nature" and asks what
government rational people would build to escape it.

| Thinker | State of nature | Why leave it | State they justify | Key work |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Thomas Hobbes (1588–1679) | "War of all against all"; life "nasty, brutish, and short" | Fear of violent death | Near-absolute sovereign; order first | *Leviathan* (1651) |
| John Locke (1632–1704) | Free and equal, but rights insecure | To protect natural rights: life, liberty, property | Limited government; right of revolution | *Two Treatises* (1689) |
| Jean-Jacques Rousseau (1712–1778) | Free but corrupted by inequality | To express the **general will** | Popular sovereignty; law as self-rule | *The Social Contract* (1762) |

The three names anchor a spectrum: Hobbes trades liberty for
security; Locke builds constitutional, limited government; Rousseau
hands sovereignty to the people themselves. Modern debates still
replay their bargain.

[warning]
Read the "state of nature" as a *thought experiment*, not history.
Its job is to show which services of the state — peace, rights, law —
no reasonable person could refuse, and therefore which powers the
state may legitimately claim.
[/warning]

## 3. Justice: Three Conceptions

| Conception | Justice is… | Institutional slogan |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Utilitarian | Whatever maximises total welfare | "The greatest good of the greatest number" |
| Deontological | Respecting each person's rights and dignity, whatever the totals | Rights are trumps, not conveniences |
| Rawlsian | What free, equal, rational people would choose behind a **veil of ignorance** | "Justice as fairness" |

John Rawls' *A Theory of Justice* (1971) rebuilt contract theory for
the modern age. Design society's basic rules without knowing your
class, talents, gender, race, or generation — which rules would you
accept? Rawls argues you would insure against bad luck, choosing:

1. **Equal basic liberties** for all — speech, conscience, political participation.
2. **Fair equality of opportunity** — offices open to all under fair conditions.
3. **The difference principle** — inequalities only when they benefit the *least advantaged*.

[example]
A hospital assigns scarce ICU beds. A strict utilitarian maximises
life-years saved; a deontologist insists triage rules treat patients
as ends, never discarding one patient to serve others; a Rawlsian
asks which rule the worst-off patient could reasonably accept.
[/example]

## 4. Liberty: Negative and Positive

**Negative liberty** is absence of interference: no one stops you
speaking, worshipping, or travelling. **Positive liberty** is the
effective power to act: literacy, health, and resources that make
rights usable. Isaiah Berlin's famous warning: tyrants have invoked
"true" positive liberty to force people to be "free".

| Idea | Claim | Champion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Harm principle | Liberty may be restricted only to prevent harm to *others* | J. S. Mill, *On Liberty* (1859) |
| Negative liberty | Freedom from interference | Berlin; classical liberals |
| Positive liberty | Freedom as self-mastery and real capability | Rousseau's heirs; Amartya Sen |

Mill's harm principle remains the sharpest tool in the box: the
state may stop you from harming others, but may not patronise you
"for your own good" — and offence, Mill insisted, is not harm.

## 5. Equality: Of What, and For What?

Equality splits into rival ideals the moment one asks *equality of
what?* — and each ideal licenses different institutions.

| Ideal | Meaning | Typical policy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Equality before the law | Same rules, same courts | Anti-discrimination law |
| Equality of opportunity | Same starting gates | Public schooling, limits on inherited advantage |
| Equality of outcome | Same ending positions | Progressive taxation, quotas |
| Equality of capabilities | Same real freedoms to be and do | Sen and Nussbaum's capability approach |

[tip]
Exam traps: Rawls *allows* inequality through the difference
principle — he is not a strict egalitarian. And "equality of
opportunity" presupposes some prior equalisation: nobody runs the
same race from different starting lines.
[/tip]

## 6. Rights: Natural, Legal, and the Two Directions

A **right** is a protected claim that overrides convenience — a
trump card, in Ronald Dworkin's image, that cannot be played away
for social gain.

| Contrast | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Natural (moral) vs legal | Rights by reason of humanity vs rights by statute | Freedom of conscience exists even where banned; a parking right exists only in law |
| Negative vs positive | Duties of forbearance vs duties to provide | Free speech vs right to education |
| Individual vs group | Held by persons vs held by peoples | Privacy vs self-determination of peoples |

The classic puzzle: if natural rights are not enacted by any
legislature, *who says they exist?* — and if they are only legal, can
a majority simply vote them away? Constitutions try to have it both
ways by entrenching rights beyond the reach of ordinary majorities.

## 7. Forms of the State

@diagram tree id="forms-of-state" layout="tree" aria="Forms of the state by who rules and by how power is distributed"
  "Forms of the state" -> "By who rules"
  "Forms of the state" -> "By structure"
  "By who rules" -> "Democracy"
  "By who rules" -> "Autocracy"
  "Democracy" -> "Direct"
  "Democracy" -> "Representative"
  "Autocracy" -> "Monarchy"
  "Autocracy" -> "Oligarchy"
  "By structure" -> "Unitary"
  "By structure" -> "Federal"
@/diagram

The two axes matter: *who rules* (democracy to autocracy) is
separate from *how power is shared* (unitary vs federal). A federal
state can be authoritarian and a unitary state deeply democratic —
the United States and the United Kingdom pair differently on the
second axis.

@chart donut title="Approximate share of world population by regime type (illustrative)" labels:
Liberal democracy
