@/chart

[info]
Figures like these — in the spirit of the V-Dem and Freedom House
reports — shift yearly. The stable lesson is that democracy's spread
is neither automatic nor irreversible; institutions are maintained
by citizens, not merely admired by them.
[/info]

## 8. Justice in the Indian Constitution

India's Preamble compresses this lesson into four promises:
**Justice** (social, economic, political), **Liberty** (of thought,
expression, belief, faith, worship), **Equality** (of status and
opportunity), and **Fraternity** — dignity of the individual and
unity of the nation.

| Value in the Preamble | Where it lives in the text |
| --- | --- |
| Justice — social, economic, political | Directive Principles (Arts. 38–39) |
| Liberty of expression and belief | Fundamental Rights (Arts. 19, 25) |
| Equality of status and opportunity | Arts. 14–18 |
| Fraternity and dignity | Arts. 17, 21 |

Note the Rawlsian echo: India guarantees *negative* liberties
("state, leave me alone") *and* positive entitlements (education,
livelihood) — because formal equality without material capability
was the founding generation's lived experience of colonial rule.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Social contract | Legitimacy from what free equals would consent to |
| Hobbes vs Locke vs Rousseau | Order first / limited government / popular sovereignty |
| Rawls | Veil of ignorance; inequalities must help the worst off |
| Harm principle | Only harm to others licenses coercion |
| Negative vs positive liberty | No interference vs real capability |
| Rights | Natural vs legal; negative vs positive; trumps, not conveniences |
